---
import SeriesHeroImage from '../../assets/ubuntu-2604-server-hero.png';
import Layout from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';

// Not a linear series. These are grouped by the job you came here to do, in the
// order you would actually hit them on a new box. Add a post to the right group
// when it goes live; keep `href` off anything unwritten.
const groups = [
	{
		name: 'Get on the box',
		blurb: 'The first twenty minutes, before it does anything useful.',
		posts: [
			{
				href: '/blog/create-sudo-user-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'Create a sudo user',
				blurb:
					'Stop using root. Plus what sudo-rs changes on this release, because the first prompt looks different enough to make you think you broke something.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/set-static-ip-ubuntu-26-04-netplan/',
				title: 'Set a static IP with Netplan',
				blurb:
					'A box you SSH into needs to stay where you left it. Server and desktop, and why the desktop GUI now writes a Netplan file too.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/install-tailscale-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'Install Tailscale',
				blurb:
					'A stable 100.x address that follows the machine around. Interactive, or with an auth key on a headless server.',
			},
		],
	},
	{
		name: 'Lock it down',
		blurb: 'Do this before the box has a public IP for long.',
		posts: [
			{
				href: '/blog/hardening-ubuntu-26-04-server/',
				title: 'Harden the server',
				blurb:
					'Keys only, firewall before you cut yourself off, unattended upgrades with a sane reboot window, and an honest look at what is actually listening.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/set-up-fail2ban-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'Set up Fail2ban',
				blurb:
					'The small second layer on port 22. This release ships Python 3.14 and a journal-first setup, so older guides point the jail at the wrong place.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/hardening-ubuntu-26-04-desktop/',
				title: 'Harden the desktop',
				blurb:
					'TPM-backed disk encryption as a first-class installer option, a Wayland-only session, per-app permissions, and where the screen lock moved in GNOME 50.',
			},
		],
	},
	{
		name: 'Run things on it',
		blurb: 'Containers, a proxy in front of them, and a GPU if you have one.',
		posts: [
			{
				href: '/blog/systemd-units-timers-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'systemd units and timers, from scratch',
				blurb:
					'One service, one timer, and everything around them measured on a real box: the one minute drift, the run missed while the box was off, what a failed job looks like, and why % and $ belong to systemd before your script sees them.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/install-docker-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'Install Docker',
				blurb:
					"From Docker's own apt repo, not the distro package, with open eyes about what adding yourself to the docker group actually hands out.",
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/caddy-reverse-proxy-docker-compose-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'Reverse proxy with Caddy',
				blurb:
					'One domain and automatic HTTPS in front of every container. The shared network, why you proxy to the container name, and the rate limit that burns a real certificate while you test.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/install-ollama-ubuntu-26-04-nvidia-gpu/',
				title: 'Install Ollama with an NVIDIA GPU',
				blurb:
					'A private LLM on your own card. The 26.04 driver and CUDA path is a couple of apt lines shorter than every 24.04 guide.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/self-host-forgejo-tailscale-docker-compose/',
				title: 'Self-host Forgejo on your tailnet',
				blurb:
					'Your own Git host with no open ports, no public DNS, and no certbot. Forgejo behind a Tailscale sidecar with real HTTPS.',
			},
		],
	},
	{
		name: 'Getting to 26.04 in the first place',
		blurb: 'And what changes underneath when you do.',
		posts: [
			{
				href: '/blog/apt-history-undo-rollback-ubuntu-26-04/',
				title: 'Undo an apt install',
				blurb:
					'apt 3.2 keeps a transaction history you can walk and reverse. What each of the five subcommands does on a real box, and the four places undo falls short.',
			},
			{
				href: '/blog/upgrade-ubuntu-24-04-to-26-04/',
				title: 'Upgrade from 24.04',
				blurb:
					'Why your box says there is no new release, the reboot that blocks the upgrade, the two different ways your repos get disabled, and a userland where ls and sort disagree on alphabetical order.',
			},
		],
	},
];

const total = groups.reduce((n, g) => n + g.posts.length, 0);
---

<Layout
	title="Ubuntu 26.04: everything I have written down"
	description="Every Ubuntu 26.04 LTS (Resolute Raccoon) guide on Peculiar Engineer, grouped by the job you came to do: getting on a new box, locking it down, running containers and a GPU on it, and upgrading to 26.04 in the first place. Tested on real machines, with the gotchas that only show up on first run."
	kind="page"
	showDate={false}
	heroImage={SeriesHeroImage}
>
	<p class="whoami"><span class="prompt">~/$</span> lsb_release -d</p>

	<p>
		Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, <strong>Resolute Raccoon</strong>, landed on 23 April 2026. It is the
		release where a few things I had been treating as fiddly extras became the default path,
		and where a few others changed quietly enough that an older guide will leave you with a
		service that starts and watches the wrong file.
	</p>

	<p>
		This is not a series you read front to back. It is {total} separate fixes, grouped by the
		job you actually came here to do. Every one was run on a real 26.04 machine, and each
		post is mostly the handful of details that are different on this release.
	</p>

	{
		groups.map((g) => (
			<section class="group">
				<h2>{g.name}</h2>
				<p class="grouplede">{g.blurb}</p>
				<ol class="parts">
					{g.posts.map((p) => (
						<li class="part done">
							<a href={p.href}>{p.title}</a>
							<p>{p.blurb}</p>
						</li>
					))}
				</ol>
			</section>
		))
	}

	<h2>What actually changed on this release</h2>

	<p>
		The short version, so you know which of your habits to distrust. <code>sudo</code> is now
		sudo-rs. Most of coreutils is a Rust reimplementation, though <code>cp</code>,{' '}
		<code>mv</code> and <code>rm</code> stayed GNU, so one box runs two implementations at once.
		<code>apt</code> is on 3.x and <code>apt-key</code> is gone entirely. Python jumped two
		versions to 3.14, which breaks every virtualenv built against 3.12. systemd is on 259 with
		cgroup v1 removed.
	</p>

	<p class="closing">
		I add to this page as I break things on new boxes. If one of these saved you an
		afternoon, that is the whole point of it.
	</p>
</Layout>

<style>
	.whoami {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		color: var(--sys-dim);
		margin-bottom: 1.5em;
	}
	.whoami .prompt {
		color: var(--sys-fg);
		font-weight: 700;
	}
	.group {
		margin-top: 2em;
	}
	.grouplede {
		color: var(--sys-dim);
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		margin: 0.2em 0 0.8em;
	}
	.parts {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0.8em 0 0;
		padding: 0;
	}
	.part {
		border: 1px solid var(--sys-dim);
		border-left: 3px solid var(--sys-fg);
		padding: 1em 1.2em;
		margin: 0 0 1em;
	}
	.part a {
		display: block;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.3;
	}
	.part p {
		margin: 0.4em 0 0;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
		color: var(--sys-text);
	}
	.closing {
		margin-top: 1.5em;
	}
</style>
